Moth uses `Int` for whole numbers and `Float` for decimal numbers.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    lives Int = 3
    progress Float = 0.75
]

An `Int` can be used where a `Float` is expected:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    lives Int = 3
    display_value Float = lives
]

A `Float` never silently becomes an `Int`. That conversion can lose
information, so it needs an explicit cast.

### The wider numeric family

The accepted design also includes `Uint`, fixed-width integers (`I8`, `I16`, `I32`,
`I64`, `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`), fixed-precision binary floats (`F16`, `F32`,
`F64`), the one-byte octet type `Byte` and the exact `Dec` family (`Dec`,
`Dec0` and `Dec1` through `Dec256`).

`Uint` follows the selected `Int` width: `Uint32` under `Int32`, `Uint64`
under `Int64`. It stays distinct from `Int` and from `U32`/`U64` even where
their widths match.

`Int`, `Uint` and `Float` stay separate types. None is a shorthand for a fixed-width
type, even when the selected width matches, because one compiler-owned numeric
profile chooses the `Int` width and the `Float` precision independently. The
default profile is `Int32` with `Float64`, and therefore `Uint32`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count Uint = 1 + 2
]

Whole literals naturally become `Int`, while a `Uint` receiver types whole-number
literal arithmetic as `Uint`. See @./#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for
that contract.

`Byte` is not a number. It holds one octet, compares as an unsigned byte value
and needs an explicit conversion to `U8` before any arithmetic. A `{Byte}` is an
ordinary collection rather than a special buffer or text type.

**Current versus accepted.** The Alpha compiler supports all four `Int`/`Float` runtime profiles, destination-aware fixed-width, `Uint` and `Byte` literals, typed fixed-width and `Uint` operators and casts, and folded numeric operations. HTML-JS executes fixed-width, `Uint`, `Byte` and `Dec` values. HTML-Wasm executes supported fixed integers, `Uint` scalars through `U32`/`U64` carriers in trap-mode-supported paths, `Byte`, `F16`, `F32` and `F64`; any operation that can actually fail and needs recoverable delivery is rejected with a structured capability diagnostic, while operations proven unable to fail, such as `U8 + U8 -> U32`, compile. Supported infallible numeric expression casts include those that do not involve `F16`, integer-to-`F16`, widening `F16` casts, `Byte -> U8` and `U8 -> Byte`. It also converts profile `Int` and fixed integers to decimal `String` values, and formats `Float`, `F16`, `F32` and `F64` text; those `Float` guards are integrity guards, not implicit numeric failure. Remaining builtin runtime casts stay gated, including fallible casts, conversions involving `Bool`, `Char` or `Error` (including `Char -> Int`), parsing from `String` and statement-form casts. Recoverable numeric failures, aggregates and reachable `Error` and `Dec` values remain Wasm-rejected. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
